docs/FAQ.md:15:It is **not** a product (no UI, no hosted service), **not** a library you import, **not** a code-completion tool, and **not** a remote controller for other repos. It does not make your agent smarter or faster — it makes failure modes visible and the ceremony on protected paths unavoidable. See [`docs/WHAT-WE-ARE.md`](WHAT-WE-ARE.md) for the long form.
docs/FAQ.md:34:- **`GOVERNANCE: ... arbitration kernel ... (hard-deny)`** — a kernel path; this needs the stronger `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` ceremony, not the ordinary sentinel.
docs/FAQ.md:36:Full catalog with fixes: [`docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md`](TROUBLESHOOTING.md). If you just want a low-friction trial without any of these blocks, install with `--ceremony user` (see Q9).
docs/FAQ.md:61:**No.** The cross-model pair-rail invokes the [Codex CLI](https://github.com/openai/codex), which is **not** bundled with this framework. On a fresh install with no Codex present, the pair-rail **fails open and contributes nothing** — protected-path edits still pass the GPG ceremony, but no second model reviews them. You only gain the cross-model rung after installing Codex yourself. This is a real limitation, called out in the README §Risks, [`docs/HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md`](HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md), and ADR-145.
docs/FAQ.md:73:Only **canonical (protected) path** edits and certain framework-structural changes. When an agent proposes an edit to a guarded path — the skill library, agent personas, ADRs, the `SPEC/`, the governance config, the enforcement-core hooks — the change is staged and requires an Owner-signed **sentinel** (a detached GPG signature over the approved change) before it lands. Routine edits to your own application code need no signature. Kernel paths (the arbitration core) need the stronger `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` ceremony rather than the ordinary sentinel. See [`PROTOCOL.md`](../PROTOCOL.md) and [`docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md`](TROUBLESHOOTING.md).
docs/FAQ.md:82:./scripts/install.sh /path/to/your-app --ceremony user
docs/FAQ.md:85:`--ceremony user` runs **advisory hooks only** — no signing ceremonies, and the installer writes only under `.claude/`. You get the audit trail and the spawn/plan structure to feel out, without the GPG friction on protected paths. Switch to the default `--ceremony maintainer` when you want the full gate. See the [README](../README.md) Quick start and [`docs/QUICKSTART.md`](QUICKSTART.md).
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:304:- **Novel canonical path** — a new canonical file is added to `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` in `check_canonical_edit.py` but the corresponding glob is NOT added to `codex-filewrite-denylist.yaml`. Gap persists until the next ceremony.
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:349:1. **`.claude/governance/codex-cli-pin-manifest.json`** ✅ **SHIPPED ADR-182** — per-targetTriple SHA-256 pin of the NATIVE Codex payload (`payloads.<triple>.{path,sha256}`) plus npm `dist.integrity` provenance for the platform package. This replaces `.claude/governance/codex-cli-binary-sha256.txt`, which is retired as a comment-only tombstone: its `shasum -a 256 $(which codex)` procedure hashed the npm JS launcher, not the executing payload (see Vulnerability (b) above). Pin update ceremony (ADR-182 §Ceremony): after a legitimate upgrade the Owner runs `python3 .claude/hooks/check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin`, transcribes the reported payload `sha256` + `target_triple` into the manifest together with the registry `dist.integrity`, and lands the edit sentinel-gated (the manifest is canonical-guarded under `.claude/governance/*.json` AND kernel-enrolled in `_KERNEL_PATHS`).
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:353:5. **Ceremony discipline** ✅ — manifest edits are sentinel-gated + kernel-guarded (see 1); pin bumps follow the ADR-182 ceremony including the ADR-111 §2 corpus reopen trigger (>5pp catch_rate shift after a CLI bump).
docs/CROSS-LLM-THREAT-MODEL.md:356:**Residual risk:** (a) hash→exec TOCTOU on the verified payload path — the window is a single `os` exec on an already-resolved absolute file, with no launcher indirection left to pivot through; accepted per ADR-182 §Residual. (b) Pin update lag after a legitimate upgrade now fails CLOSED at the first L3+ invocation (a loud block naming the re-pin ceremony) instead of passing silently — the failure mode moved from invisible-compromise to visible-friction by design. (c) Only triples enumerated in the manifest can verify; hosts whose triple is absent fail CLOSED rather than degrade to advisory. There is no runtime skip env var for this pin (the previously-documented `CEO_PAIR_RAIL_SKIP_BINARY_PIN` intent was never implemented and is dropped). `CEO_PAIR_RAIL_CODEX_BIN` is NOT a verification bypass — a set override is itself hashed and verified against the manifest (mismatch fails CLOSED); the manifest/triple seams are honoured only under `CEO_PAIR_RAIL_TEST_MODE=1` and ignored on the live path. The only true bypass is the global `CEO_PAIR_RAIL_DISABLE=1` kill-switch (audited).
